---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import TrapFigure from '../../components/TrapFigure.astro';
import { trapFacts } from '../../sim/trap-facts.js';

/** Numbers about Fig. 1 come from the run the band replays. */
const f = trapFacts();
const fmt = (x: number) => Math.round(x).toLocaleString('en-US');

const TITLE = 'Inside the Trap: A Renewal Law for Prefix-Cache Collapse in LLM Agent Serving';
const ARXIV = 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08902';
const REPO = 'https://github.com/AkshatValse/surviving-llm-stampede';

/** The abstract as posted on arXiv, verbatim. */
const ABSTRACT = `Prefix caching lets an LLM agent's follow-up turns reuse cached KV, but a burst can tip an engine into a trap in which every turn misses and the backlog does not drain, at a load it served before. In registered paired tests on two engines, two GPUs and two models (passed at 9 of 10 rates), a 200-session burst left the engine at zero hits to the end in all 30 runs, while every matched no-burst run stayed good and a session cap prevented the trap in all 30 capped runs. We analyse an idealization of the trapped state exactly: for a fluid FIFO server on which every turn misses, we prove that along FIFO orbits its cycles obey a linear renewal recursion. As is classical, the trap drains if and only if arrivals are below the all-miss capacity; above it, a Perron root fixes the dynamics: every trapped state converges to a known self-similar regime in which the gap between turns grows linearly and the turns served are young and short. For our workload the backlog grows at 0.6 to 0.8 of the excess load and sessions complete faster than the all-miss capacity, a youth premium with turn-count and cost-growth factors, so in the model a trap's measured throughput overstates the recovery threshold. Retrospectively, with nothing fitted to trap data, the theory matches 57 stuck runs: the mean served prompt within 3% in 50 runs, completions above the all-miss capacity in every run, and the pooled gap growth. Blind in outcome, 4 runs trapped by a tool slowdown met 4 of 5 pre-set scores, cut traps persisted, and with a 20-step budget, trapped runs completed below the all-miss capacity, as predicted. Of two exits, refusing arrivals sheds sessions; a session cap restores reuse: in registered tests it rescued 9 of 9 established traps at the same load without shedding, and the engine stayed good after release. We do not prove that the engine converges to the model.`;

/** Identical to the Citation section of the repository README. */
const BIBTEX = `@misc{valse2026inside,
  title         = {Inside the Trap: A Renewal Law for Prefix-Cache Collapse in {LLM} Agent Serving},
  author        = {Valse, Akshat and Jannesari, Ali},
  year          = {2026},
  eprint        = {2611.08902},
  archivePrefix = {arXiv},
  primaryClass  = {cs.DC},
  url           = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08902}
}`;

const ISU = { '@type': 'CollegeOrUniversity', name: 'Iowa State University' };
---

<Base
  title="Inside the Trap · Akshat Valse"
  description="Inside the Trap (arXiv:2611.08902): why a burst of agent traffic can trap a prefix-cached LLM serving engine, a renewal law for the trapped state, and what gets the engine out."
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[
    {
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'ScholarlyArticle',
      headline: TITLE,
      name: TITLE,
      author: [
        { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Akshat Valse', url: new URL('/', Astro.site).href, affiliation: ISU },
        { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Ali Jannesari', affiliation: ISU },
      ],
      datePublished: '2026-11-06',
      abstract: ABSTRACT,
      keywords: ['LLM serving', 'prefix caching', 'KV cache', 'LLM agents', 'metastable failures', 'admission control', 'queueing'],
      url: new URL('/research/inside-the-trap', Astro.site).href,
      sameAs: ARXIV,
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
    },
  ]}
>
  <TrapFigure slot="hero" />

  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="rail">
        <dl>
          <dt>Preprint</dt><dd>arXiv:2611.08902</dd>
          <dt>Subjects</dt><dd>cs.DC · cs.PF</dd>
          <dt>Posted</dt><dd>November 2026</dd>
        </dl>
        <hr class="rail-rule" />
        <dl>
          <dt>Bears on</dt>
          <dd>LLM serving · prefix caching · KV cache · agent workloads</dd>
        </dl>
        <hr class="rail-rule" />
        <span class="rail-label">Fig. 1</span>
        <div class="legend" aria-hidden="true">
          <span>0</span><span class="legend-ramp"></span><span>1</span>
        </div>
        <p class="legend-note">Color: cache hit fraction</p>
        <p class="legend-note">Height: sessions, √ scale</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h1 class="page-title">
          Inside the Trap: <span class="subtitle">A Renewal Law for Prefix-Cache Collapse in LLM Agent Serving</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="entry-meta">
          Akshat Valse and Ali Jannesari · Department of Computer Science, Iowa State
          University · arXiv:2611.08902, November 2026 · 66 pages
        </p>
        <dl class="links">
          <dt>Paper</dt>
          <dd><a href={ARXIV}>arXiv abstract</a> · <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08902">PDF</a></dd>
          <dt>Code and data</dt>
          <dd><a href={REPO}>surviving-llm-stampede</a></dd>
          <dt>Release</dt>
          <dd><a href={`${REPO}/tree/trap-arxiv-v1`}>trap-arxiv-v1</a>, the commit the preprint describes</dd>
          <dt>Cite</dt>
          <dd><a href="#cite">BibTeX</a></dd>
        </dl>

        <h2>In brief</h2>
        <p>
          Why can an LLM serving engine stay stuck after a burst of agent traffic has
          passed, at a load it served before the burst?
        </p>
        <p>
          An agent works in a loop: model call, tool call, model call, re-sending the
          whole growing conversation each turn. Serving engines such as SGLang and vLLM
          keep that shared prefix in a GPU KV cache, so a follow-up turn whose prefix is
          still cached prefills only its new tokens; in the paper's workload a hit
          prefills 551 tokens and a miss about 11,100. The cached KV belongs to sessions
          that sit idle in tool calls, and admitting waiting work evicts it. A burst of
          new sessions can therefore tip the engine into a trap in which every turn
          misses and the backlog does not drain.
        </p>
        <p>
          In registered paired tests on Iowa State's Nova cluster, on two engines, two
          GPUs and two models, a 200-session burst left the engine at zero hits to the
          end in all 30 runs (the registered rule was met at 9 of 10 rates), every
          matched no-burst run stayed good, and a client-side session cap prevented the
          trap in all 30 capped runs.
        </p>
        <p>
          The paper then asks what the trapped state is. It idealizes that state as a
          fluid FIFO server on which every turn misses and proves that along FIFO orbits
          its cycles obey a linear renewal recursion. The trap drains if and only if
          arrivals are below the all-miss capacity; above it, every trapped state
          converges to a known self-similar regime in which the turns served are young
          and short. For the workload studied, sessions then complete faster than the
          all-miss capacity, so in the model a trap's measured throughput overstates the
          load at which it drains. With nothing fitted to trap data, the theory matches
          57 stuck runs retrospectively, and it was also tested blind in outcome.
        </p>
        <p>
          Of two ways out, refusing arrivals sheds sessions, while a session cap
          restores cache reuse: in registered tests it rescued 9 of 9 established traps
          at the same load without shedding a session. The paper does not prove that
          the engine converges to the model.
        </p>

        <h2>The figure above</h2>
        <p>
          Fig. 1 runs the paper’s simulator in your browser from a fixed seed: the engine
          calibrated to SGLang on an A100 with a 300,000-token KV pool, iteration by
          iteration. Each lane’s height is the number of sessions in the system, on a
          square-root scale, and its color is the fraction of follow-up turns served from
          the prefix cache, from 0 to 1. Both lanes get the same agent traffic: a Poisson
          stream of {fmt(f.baseSessions)} sessions at {f.mult} times the measured all-miss
          rate, plus a burst of {f.burstN} at {fmt(f.tB)} s. The top engine has no cap and
          stays at zero cache hits to the end. The bottom one gets the paper’s rescue cap
          of {f.cap} sessions at {fmt(f.engage)} s: its hits come back by
          {fmt(f.hitsBack)} s, drop out again while the gate drains the backlog, and hold
          from {fmt(f.hitsHold)} s, and the cap comes off at {fmt(f.release)} s. Over the
          last 3,000 s the trapped engine’s turns average {fmt(f.trapped.meanPrompt)}
          prompt tokens, against {fmt(f.model.Pz)} in model F and {fmt(f.model.Pstat)} in
          the normal turn mix. The site’s checks run the same code against the paper’s
          Python engine and get identical counts.
        </p>
        <p>
          This run was chosen from {f.selection.candidates} seeds as one in which every
          burst session finishes before the end, the cap comes off inside the paper’s
          engine range, and the completion rate is closest to model F’s. Over
          {f.further.seeds} further seeds that played no part in the choice, every engine
          that gets the burst and no cap is stuck at zero hits, the trapped engines’ served
          prompts average {f.further.promptOverPz.toFixed(2)} times model F’s, and their
          completion rates average {f.further.completionsOverMiss.toFixed(2)} times the
          all-miss capacity, from {f.further.completionsMin.toFixed(2)} to
          {f.further.completionsMax.toFixed(2)}.
        </p>

        <h2>Abstract</h2>
        <p>{ABSTRACT}</p>

        <h2>Code, data and registrations</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={REPO}>The repository</a> holds the simulator, the real-engine load
          generator, the analysis code, all real-engine data with its job logs, and every
          registration. Each number in the paper traces to a file and a commit or Slurm
          job. The README shows the paper's Fig. 2 with its caption, and explains how to
          rebuild the tables and figures from a fresh clone.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every registration and blind prediction in the paper was committed and pushed
          to GitHub before the data it concerns was read, except where the paper's
          Appendix E.12 says otherwise. One command, run from the root of a clone,
          checks them against the commits GitHub's push log recorded:
        </p>
        <pre><code>python provenance/registration-proofs/verify.py</code></pre>

        <h2 id="cite">Citation</h2>
        <pre><code>{BIBTEX}</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-title { line-height: 1.15; }
  .subtitle {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    font-family: var(--font-body), Georgia, serif;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-variation-settings: normal;
    line-height: 1.32;
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
  }
  .links { margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
</style>
